 "verdict": "host conditioning adds a REAL but WEAK and INCONSISTENT defence against ordinary-to-victim traffic on real in-distribution data: it fires zero on ~half the orgs (median delta -0.0000) but materially more on 1/8 (host mean benign->victim 0.0114 vs flow-only 0.0002, ~73x; max 0.089). This is 5-38x BELOW the LSPR23 grafted 0.43, so the grafted stress-window firing was an OUT-OF-DISTRIBUTION OVERESTIMATE, and host conditioning is not the strong defence it appeared -- padding substantially transfers. [MODERATE regime; TCP flows; AIT has no high-volume flood, which remains open]",
 "note": "R7 on AIT (TCP flows): benign-inclusive so the padding-transfer question is measured on REAL benign-to-victim flows, not a graft (no OOD). Cross-scenario detector (train 7 orgs, test 1), in-scenario benign calibration, per-fold feature selection with absolute-time fields excluded, unknown/empty labels dropped. The load-bearing claim: host conditioning adds a real but WEAK, INCONSISTENT effect over flow-only against ordinary-to-victim traffic (host mean ~1% vs flow ~0.02%, zero on ~half the orgs, max ~8%), which is 6-38x below the LSPR23 grafted 43% -- so the graft was an OOD OVERESTIMATE and padding substantially transfers. Moderate (recon/web-exploit) regime; no high-volume flood."
